{
  "id": "T18_58_in_a_participant_perspective_a_normalization_counts_once_its_issuance_is_distributed",
  "family": "T18",
  "rules": [
    "7.3.8",
    "4.6.1",
    "4.4.2",
    "7.6.14",
    "9.4.2",
    "9.7.2"
  ],
  "description": "A fire-rating story (\"90 min\" against \"1.5 h\") is recorded, and both statements were distributed to the consultant. The owner's normalization of the project takes effect at Wednesday's meeting, and is recorded from the minutes, which are issued to the consultant at 18:00. At a Wednesday 15:00 cutoff the record view shows the story ended by the normalization, while the consultant's view, in which the normalization has not yet been distributed, shows it open and disagreeing and the conflicts read reports the literal difference. From Thursday the normalization counts in the consultant's view and applies from its own effective time: the story reads ended on Wednesday at 10:00, and the pair reads as matched.",
  "note": "7.3.8: \"In a participant perspective, a normalization recorded from a communication counts only once that communication's issuance has been distributed to the participant, as any such act does (4.6.1). It then applies from its own effective time, and the interpretation in force in that perspective is the one these rules give from the normalizations that count in it.\" 4.6.1: \"Begin and end acts are not read in a participant perspective; reads compute the periods in it (7.6.14). A distributed act applies at its own effective time.\"",
